# McRogueFace AddressSanitizer Suppression File
#
# CPython false positives are handled by:
#   - PYTHONMALLOC=malloc (bypasses pymalloc)
#   - LSAN suppressions below (CPython has intentional lifetime leaks)
#
# Usage (via ASAN_OPTIONS or LSAN_OPTIONS):
#   LSAN_OPTIONS="suppressions=sanitizers/asan.supp"
#
# Format: one suppression per line, prefix with "leak:" for leak suppressions
# See https://github.com/google/sanitizers/wiki/AddressSanitizerLeakSanitizer

# CPython interned strings — intentionally never freed
leak:_PyUnicode_InternInPlace
leak:PyUnicode_InternFromString

# CPython type objects — intentionally immortal
leak:PyType_Ready
leak:_PyType_Ready

# CPython small int cache ([-5, 256]) — allocated once, never freed
leak:_PyLong_Init

# CPython GIL — allocated once per interpreter
leak:PyThread_allocate_lock

# CPython import system caches
leak:PyImport_ImportModule
leak:_PyImport_FindExtensionObject

# dlopen — loaded shared libraries are intentionally kept resident
leak:dlopen
leak:_dl_open

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Definition-time leftovers (CPython 3.15).
#
# Why anything survives Py_Finalize at all: the engine keeps Python objects
# alive past it (the enum classes cached in Py*::*_enum_class statics, and
# any object still owned by C++ when finalization starts -- see the note at
# the bottom). Each surviving object strands what it references: its class,
# that class's module, the module's code objects. A bare embedder running
# the same code with no such references leaks nothing (verified 2026-10).
#
# These frames only run when code is *defined* (parsed, unmarshalled, a class
# or module body executed), never per engine operation, so they cannot hide
# a leak that grows with use. Anchored (^...$) because LSan matches a bare
# template as a substring of every frame's function AND file name.
#
# Never add generic allocation frames here (new_keys_object, list_resize,
# PyType_GenericAlloc, _PyObject_New...): engine-caused leaks allocate
# through them too, and LSan suppresses a report if ANY frame matches.
#
# Caveat: freelist-recycled objects (tuples, lists, dicts, floats) carry the
# stack of the chunk's first malloc. A per-op engine leak of one can hide
# behind an init-time stack for at most a freelist's worth of iterations;
# measure growth at N=10 vs N=40, not presence at N=1.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# .pyc unmarshalling (code objects and their constants)
leak:^r_object$
# compiling source: parser and compiler
leak:^_PyPegen_
leak:^_PyAST_Compile$
# class creation: the type object, its dict, its __doc__, __set_name__ work
leak:^type_new_impl$
leak:^builtin___build_class__$
# class attributes assigned after creation (enum's EnumType, Key.__hash__ = ...)
leak:^type_setattro$
# BLIND SPOT: these also hide engine leaks caused while an imported module's body
# runs (a game module that builds scenes at import). Test scripts run via --exec
# (PyRun, not exec()), so their bodies stay checked.
# module bodies: importlib runs a source module's body via exec(), frozen
# modules via exec_code_in_module, C extensions via PyModule_ExecDef;
# extension module objects are built by PyModule_FromDefAndSpec2
leak:^builtin_exec$
leak:^exec_code_in_module$
leak:^PyModule_ExecDef$
leak:^PyModule_FromDefAndSpec2$
leak:^_add_methods_to_object$

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Engine leftovers that exist once per process.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Interpreter startup (Py_InitializeFromConfig and the frozen importlib).
leak:McRFPy_API::init_python_with_config
# mcrfpy is created once per process (Py_mod_create refuses a second time).
# Everything built here lives as long as the interpreter: the static types,
# the mcrfpy.automation submodule, and the IntEnum classes from
# Py*::create_enum_class, which the *_enum_class statics hold a strong
# reference to and never release, so they outlive Py_Finalize.
leak:mcrfpy_module_create

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT suppressible here: objects still owned by C++ at Py_Finalize.
#
# CPython clears runtime->initialized before it collects garbage and clears
# module dicts, so during finalization Py_IsInitialized() is already 0 and
# every engine DECREF guarded by it is skipped (PyIdentityPin::release,
# ~Animation, ~UIEntity, ~UIGridView). A Frame with children, a Grid with
# entities, a running Timer or a registered Scene that is still alive when
# the script calls sys.exit() strands its C++ half. These allocate through
# ordinary constructors (UICaption::init, UIGridView::init, PyTimer::init...),
# so suppressing them would also hide per-op leaks of the same objects. The
# same objects released before exit leak nothing. Fix it in the engine
# (tear the engine's Python references down before Py_Finalize), not here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
